+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report for `spade`
+
+Property-based tests were added with [hegel](https://hegel.dev) (`hegeltest` 0.28.2, added as a
+dev-dependency). All tests live in the existing unit test modules next to the code they test.
+Shared draw helpers live in `src/test_utilities.rs` (`draw_valid_coordinate`, `draw_valid_point`,
+`draw_valid_points`, `position_key`). Coordinates are drawn from a mix of regimes: a small integer
+grid (degeneracy-rich: duplicate, collinear and cocircular points), moderate float ranges, the full
+documented coordinate range and the exact documented boundary values
+`MIN_ALLOWED_VALUE`/`MAX_ALLOWED_VALUE` (spade documents that coordinate magnitudes must be zero or
+within `[2^-142, 2^201]`).
+
+**Four real bugs were found**, all in the flood-fill shape iterators
+(`get_vertices_in_rectangle` / `get_vertices_in_circle`, `src/flood_fill_iterator.rs`), all
+violations of documented behavior on inputs well within the documented coordinate limits. Per
+instructions, no bug was fixed. Three are pinned by deterministically failing tests; the fourth
+(an infinite loop) is documented by an `#[ignore]`d reproducer because running it would hang the
+whole suite.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+### `src/delaunay_core/triangulation_ext.rs`
+
+1. **`hegel_delaunay_empty_circumcircle`** — the defining global Delaunay invariant: no vertex lies
+   strictly inside the circumcircle of any inner face.
+   *Oracle:* exact predicates from the `robust` crate (`robust::incircle`), applied to **all**
+   vertex/face pairs — a stronger, independent check than the crate's own `sanity_check`, which
+   only tests adjacent triangle pairs.
+   *Evidence:* crate docs ("Uses exact geometric predicate evaluation"); `robust` is spade's own
+   dependency, per the skill's "dependencies as oracle" guidance.
+
+2. **`hegel_insertion_order_invariance`** — `bulk_load` and incremental insertion in an arbitrary
+   drawn order produce the same vertex set, the same convex-hull edge set, and the same edge/face
+   counts. (Edge *sets* may legitimately differ for cocircular inputs, but the counts are uniquely
+   determined by V and the hull; the test deliberately compares only order-independent artifacts.)
+   *Evidence:* `bulk_load` documented as an equivalent, faster alternative to `insert`.
+
+3. **`hegel_euler_formula_after_insertions_and_removals`** — after an arbitrary drawn sequence of
+   `insert`/`remove` operations: `V − E + F = 2` (including the outer face), or `F = 1`,
+   `E = V − 1` when all vertices are collinear.
+   *Evidence:* documented on `Triangulation::new`/`num_all_faces` and in `basic_sanity_check`.
+
+4. **`hegel_locate_agrees_with_exact_predicates`** — `locate()` results verified against
+   `robust::orient2d`: `OnVertex` matches the position exactly; `OnEdge` points lie exactly on the
+   edge's segment; `OnFace` points lie strictly inside the face; `OutsideOfConvexHull` points are
+   contained in no inner face and the returned edge borders the outer face with the query on its
+   left (as documented); `NoTriangulation` only for ≤ 1 vertex. Queries are boosted with exact
+   vertex positions and vertex-pair midpoints to reach the `OnVertex`/`OnEdge` branches.
+   *Evidence:* `PositionInTriangulation` variant documentation.
+
+5. **`hegel_delaunay_matches_point_set_model`** — stateful model test (`#[hegel::state_machine]`):
+   a `DelaunayTriangulation` against a `BTreeMap` keyed by exact position, with rules
+   `insert` (duplicate positions overwrite, as documented), `remove_existing`
+   (`locate_and_remove` returns the removed vertex), `locate_arbitrary` (`locate_vertex` agrees
+   with model membership), and a drawn `check_structure` rule running the crate's full
+   `sanity_check`; invariant: `num_vertices == model.len()`.
+
+### `src/delaunay_core/math.rs`
+
+6. **`hegel_insert_agrees_with_validate_vertex`** — the coordinate-limit contract: `insert`
+   succeeds exactly when `validate_vertex` returns `Ok`, returns the identical error otherwise,
+   never panics, and leaves the triangulation unchanged on failure (documented on `insert`).
+   Inputs: arbitrary `f64` including NaN/±∞, boosted with the exact boundary values
+   (`MIN_ALLOWED_VALUE`, `MAX_ALLOWED_VALUE`, valid) and their nearest neighbors just outside the
+   allowed range (`MIN_ALLOWED_VALUE.next_down()`, `MAX_ALLOWED_VALUE.next_up()`, invalid).
+
+7. **`hegel_mitigate_underflow_never_too_small`** — a position returned by `mitigate_underflow`
+   never causes `InsertionError::TooSmall` (documented), and the function only ever flushes
+   underflow-band coordinates to zero, passing everything else through unchanged (including NaN).
+
+### `src/cdt.rs`
+
+8. **`hegel_cdt_try_add_constraint_chain`** — for a CDT built from drawn points and drawn
+   constraint attempts: `try_add_constraint` returns an empty list exactly when `from == to` or
+   `can_add_constraint` is false; otherwise the returned edges form a connected chain of
+   constraint edges from `from` to `to` (splitting at exactly-collinear vertices is exercised by
+   the integer-grid point regime); `num_constraints()` always equals the number of edges flagged
+   `is_constraint_edge`; `cdt_sanity_check()` passes afterwards.
+   *Evidence:* documentation of `try_add_constraint`, `can_add_constraint`, `num_constraints`.
+
+### `src/flood_fill_iterator.rs`
+
+9. **`hegel_vertices_in_rectangle_match_brute_force`** — `get_vertices_in_rectangle` returns
+   exactly the vertices with `lower ≤ p ≤ upper` component-wise (boundary included), without
+   duplicates. *Oracle:* brute-force filter over all vertices. Rectangle corners are boosted to
+   coincide with drawn vertex coordinates so vertices frequently lie exactly on the boundary.
+   Points and corners are drawn as exact small-integer multiples of one power-of-two scale per
+   test case, sweeping the full documented magnitude range; inputs mixing vastly different
+   magnitudes and inverted corners are the pinned bug classes #1/#3 and are excluded here with
+   reference comments. The hull-walk hang class (bug #2) is excluded via the semantic
+   `hull_walk_may_hang` guard. **This test found bugs #1 and #3.**
+
+10. **`hegel_vertices_in_circle_match_brute_force`** — `get_vertices_in_circle` returns exactly
+    the vertices with `distance²(center, p) ≤ radius_2` (boundary included), without duplicates.
+    `radius_2` is boosted with the exact squared distance to a drawn vertex, putting that vertex
+    exactly on the circle boundary. Same input regime and exclusions as (9).
+    **This test found bugs #2 and #4.**
+
+### Pinned bug tests (kept failing / ignored on purpose)
+
+11. **`hegel_inverted_rectangle_yields_empty_iterator`** — KNOWN FAILURE, pins bug #1; fails
+    deterministically on every run and every drawn input.
+12. **`known_bug_hull_walk_hangs_on_distant_shapes`** — `#[ignore]`d reproducer for bug #2
+    (running it hangs the process; there is no per-test timeout).
+13. **`hegel_rectangle_query_includes_vertex_left_of_extreme_rectangle`** — KNOWN FAILURE, pins
+    bug #3 deterministically for every exponent 54..=201.
+14. **`known_bug_circle_query_misses_boundary_vertex`** — KNOWN FAILURE (plain `#[test]` with the
+    exact hegel-shrunk input, because the failure depends on the DCEL's deterministic edge
+    orientation for that construction), pins bug #4.
+
+## Test results
+
+Command: `cargo test -p spade --lib hegel_` (plus `known_bug_circle_query_misses_boundary_vertex`)
+
+```
+ok      delaunay_core::math::test::hegel_mitigate_underflow_never_too_small
+ok      delaunay_core::math::test::hegel_insert_agrees_with_validate_vertex
+ok      cdt::test::hegel_cdt_try_add_constraint_chain
+ok      delaunay_core::triangulation_ext::test::hegel_locate_agrees_with_exact_predicates
+ok      delaunay_core::triangulation_ext::test::hegel_euler_formula_after_insertions_and_removals
+ok      delaunay_core::triangulation_ext::test::hegel_insertion_order_invariance
+ok      delaunay_core::triangulation_ext::test::hegel_delaunay_empty_circumcircle
+ok      delaunay_core::triangulation_ext::test::hegel_delaunay_matches_point_set_model
+ok      flood_fill_iterator::test::hegel_vertices_in_rectangle_match_brute_force
+ok      flood_fill_iterator::test::hegel_vertices_in_circle_match_brute_force
+FAILED  flood_fill_iterator::test::hegel_inverted_rectangle_yields_empty_iterator      (pins bug #1)
+FAILED  flood_fill_iterator::test::hegel_rectangle_query_includes_vertex_left_of_extreme_rectangle (pins bug #3)
+FAILED  flood_fill_iterator::test::known_bug_circle_query_misses_boundary_vertex       (pins bug #4)
+ignored flood_fill_iterator::test::known_bug_hull_walk_hangs_on_distant_shapes         (bug #2 hangs the process)
+```
+
+- **Liveness check:** temporarily inverting the Euler-formula assertion (`e + 3`) produced a
+  failure that hegel shrank to the minimal triangle `V = 3, E = 3, F = 2`; the change was
+  reverted.
+- **Exploratory 10× runs:** all ten live properties were additionally run with
+  `test_cases = 1000` (then reverted to the default 100). The first 10× run is what surfaced
+  bugs #3 and #4; after pinning them the 10× runs are clean.
+- **Pin determinism:** the three failing pin tests were run three times from a clean state
+  (failure database removed); all three failed on every run.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+All four bugs are in the flood-fill shape iterator machinery backing
+`FloatTriangulation::get_vertices_in_rectangle` / `get_vertices_in_circle` (and the edge
+variants share the same machinery). All inputs are well within the documented
+`MIN_ALLOWED_VALUE`/`MAX_ALLOWED_VALUE` coordinate limits.
+
+### Bug #1: `get_vertices_in_rectangle` returns vertices for inverted (empty) rectangles
+
+- **Contract:** "Yields an empty iterator if `lower.x > upper.x || lower.y > upper.y`."
+- **Counterexample (hegel-shrunk, minimal):** a triangulation containing the single vertex
+  `(0, 0)`, queried with `lower = (0, 0)`, `upper = (0, -1)` → returns the vertex.
+- **Root cause:** `VerticesInShapeIterator` tests containment via the `DistanceMetric` **trait's**
+  default `is_point_inside` (`distance_to_point(p) <= 0`). `RectangleMetric::distance_to_point`
+  measures the distance to the rectangle's four frame edges, which is `0` for a point lying on
+  the frame — even when the rectangle is inverted and hence empty. `RectangleMetric` has an
+  **inherent** `is_point_inside` (exact component-wise comparison) that answers correctly, but it
+  does not override the trait method, so generic code never calls it
+  (`src/flood_fill_iterator.rs`: trait default near line 36, inherent method near line 151).
+- **Pinned by:** `hegel_inverted_rectangle_yields_empty_iterator`.
+
+### Bug #2: infinite loop for distant query shapes (process hang)
+
+- **Reproducer (verified to hang):**
+  ```rust
+  let t = DelaunayTriangulation::<Point2<f64>>::bulk_load(vec![
+      Point2::new(0.0, 0.0), Point2::new(1.0, 0.0), Point2::new(0.0, 1.0),
+  ]).unwrap();
+  t.get_vertices_in_circle(Point2::new(0.5, spade::MAX_ALLOWED_VALUE), 1.0).count(); // never returns
+  ```
+- **Root cause:** `FloodFillIterator::get_start_edges` (the `OutsideOfConvexHull` arm, around
+  line 383) walks the convex hull and terminates only when the distance to the shape **strictly**
+  increases (`next_distance > min_distance`) or a hull edge overlaps the shape. When the shape is
+  so far away that the `f64` squared distances of all hull vertices round to the same value (here
+  every distance is `MAX_ALLOWED_VALUE²`), the walk cycles the hull forever. The same
+  all-distances-equal condition can also arise geometrically (e.g. a small query circle centered
+  at the circumcenter of an obtuse grid triangle), so this is not exclusively an extreme-magnitude
+  problem. Found by hegel; the hang initially looked like a 10+ minute shrink.
+- **Documented by:** `known_bug_hull_walk_hangs_on_distant_shapes` (`#[ignore]`d — a
+  failing-by-hanging test would take the whole suite down). The live tests exclude exactly this
+  class via the semantic `hull_walk_may_hang` helper ("all hull distances equal, positive, and no
+  hull edge overlaps").
+
+### Bug #3: rectangle query includes a vertex strictly outside the rectangle (precision loss)
+
+- **Contract:** `get_vertices_in_rectangle` returns "all vertices in a rectangle".
+- **Counterexample (hegel-shrunk):** vertex `(0, -15)`, rectangle `lower = (1, -15)`,
+  `upper = (2.9802e60, -15)` → the vertex (with `x = 0 < 1`) is returned.
+- **Root cause:** distance-based containment again (bug #1's mechanism), now triggered by
+  catastrophic cancellation: for the frame edge from `(2^k, y)` to `(1, y)` with `k ≥ 54`,
+  `fl(1 − 2^k) = −2^k`, so `nearest_point` computes the projection foot as exactly `(0, y)` — the
+  query point itself — and the computed distance is `0` instead of `1`.
+- **Pinned by:** `hegel_rectangle_query_includes_vertex_left_of_extreme_rectangle`
+  (deterministic for every drawn exponent 54..=201 and every grid `y`).
+
+### Bug #4: circle query misses a vertex lying exactly on the circle boundary (precision loss)
+
+- **Contract:** `get_vertices_in_circle`: "Any vertex on the circle's boundary is also returned."
+- **Counterexample (hegel-shrunk):** vertices `(0, 1)` and `(0, −2^53)`; circle centered exactly
+  on `(0, −2^53)` with `radius_2 = 0` → returns nothing instead of the center vertex.
+- **Root cause:** the flood fill only starts from edges overlapping the shape
+  (`CircleMetric::is_edge_inside` → inexact `math::distance_2`). Projecting the center
+  `(0, −2^53)` onto the edge from `(0, 1)` to `(0, −2^53)` absorbs the `+1`
+  (`fl(−2^53 − 1) = −2^53`), the projection foot rounds to `(0, 1 − 2^53)`, the computed edge
+  distance is `1 > 0`, no start edge is found, and the iterator yields nothing — even though the
+  vertex containment predicate itself (`center.distance_2(p) ≤ radius_2`) is satisfied exactly.
+- **Pinned by:** `known_bug_circle_query_misses_boundary_vertex`.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **Edge-set equality for insertion-order invariance.** The stronger property "same point set ⇒
+  same undirected edge set" is unsound: for cocircular point sets (which the integer-grid
+  generator produces on purpose) the Delaunay triangulation is not unique, so `bulk_load` and
+  incremental insertion may legally triangulate a cocircular quad differently. Weakened to
+  vertex-set/hull-edge-set/count equality, which is order-independent even in degenerate cases.
+- **Strict endpoint-exclusion for `OnEdge` locate results** (a query equal to an endpoint should
+  arguably be `OnVertex`): not clearly promised by the docs, so the test only asserts
+  collinearity plus containment in the closed bounding box of the edge.
+- **Mixed-magnitude inputs in the two live shape-query tests** were not abandoned as *unsound* —
+  the properties are correct and the code fails them — but after each failure class was pinned by
+  its own canonical bug test, the live tests were restricted to scale-homogeneous grid inputs
+  (drawn per-case power-of-two scale sweeping `2^-134..2^193`) so they stay green and keep
+  checking everything else. Each exclusion carries a comment referencing the pinning test.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The workflow worked end-to-end; all four bugs were found by exactly the tactics the skill
+  recommends: probing documented boundary values, using the crate's own dependency (`robust`) as
+  an exact oracle, boosting boundary conjunctions (corners/radii snapped to vertex coordinates),
+  and the mandatory 10× exploratory run — bugs #3 and #4 were invisible at the default 100 cases
+  and appeared only at 1000.
+- The warning that "a hang looks exactly like slow shrinking" was accurate and important: the
+  circle test ran 10+ minutes and only an `eprintln!` instrumentation run (as suggested in
+  "Keeping a Failing Test That Pins a Real Bug") revealed the hanging input. A per-test
+  wall-clock budget in hegel, or a documented idiom for one, would shorten this loop a lot.
+- `draw_N` labels in counterexample output are positional only; mapping them back to the drawing
+  code takes care. An optional label argument on `tc.draw` would help.
+- The skill's advice to keep pinned failures *deterministic* shaped the final design (pin tests
+  drawing only from input families where every draw provably fails). A worked example of this
+  pattern in the skill would be useful.
+- Guidance that would have saved a round-trip: when a brute-force-oracle property over a
+  numerically inexact subsystem fails at extreme magnitudes, the fix is often to make the *live*
+  generator scale-homogeneous (constructively) rather than to filter with `tc.assume`, which
+  would have rejected nearly every mixed-regime case (`FilterTooMuch`).
+- Minor reference-doc confirmations: in a `#![no_std]` crate with the `std` feature enabled, the
+  documented `use std::{format, string::ToString};` fix for `#[hegel::test]` worked as described;
+  `one_of!` needing the `vec!` macro in scope was also correctly documented.
diff --git a/src/cdt.rs b/src/cdt.rs
index a2204e3..b782618 100644
--- a/src/cdt.rs
+++ b/src/cdt.rs
@@ -2663,4 +2663,67 @@ mod test {
 
         }
     }
+
+    // === Hegel property based tests ===
+
+    use crate::test_utilities::draw_valid_points;
+    use hegel::generators::{self as gens};
+    use std::{format, string::ToString};
+
+    /// `try_add_constraint` behaves as documented:
+    ///  - it returns an empty list exactly if `from == to` or the constraint would cross an
+    ///    existing constraint edge (which `can_add_constraint` reports),
+    ///  - otherwise it returns a chain of constraint edges leading from `from` to `to`,
+    ///  - `num_constraints` always equals the number of edges flagged as constraint edges,
+    ///  - the CDT stays structurally valid (`cdt_sanity_check`).
+    #[hegel::test]
+    fn hegel_cdt_try_add_constraint_chain(tc: hegel::TestCase) {
+        let points = draw_valid_points(&tc, 20);
+        let mut cdt = Cdt::new();
+        for point in points {
+            cdt.insert(point).unwrap();
+        }
+
+        let handles: Vec<FixedVertexHandle> = cdt.fixed_vertices().collect();
+        if !handles.is_empty() {
+            let num_attempts = tc.draw(gens::integers::<usize>().max_value(10));
+            for _ in 0..num_attempts {
+                let from = handles[tc.draw(gens::integers::<usize>().max_value(handles.len() - 1))];
+                let to = handles[tc.draw(gens::integers::<usize>().max_value(handles.len() - 1))];
+
+                if from == to {
+                    assert!(cdt.try_add_constraint(from, to).is_empty());
+                    continue;
+                }
+
+                let could_add = cdt.can_add_constraint(from, to);
+                let edges = cdt.try_add_constraint(from, to);
+                assert_eq!(edges.is_empty(), !could_add);
+
+                if !edges.is_empty() {
+                    // The returned edges form a connected chain of constraint edges
+                    // from `from` to `to`.
+                    assert_eq!(cdt.directed_edge(edges[0]).from().fix(), from);
+                    assert_eq!(cdt.directed_edge(*edges.last().unwrap()).to().fix(), to);
+                    for pair in edges.windows(2) {
+                        assert_eq!(
+                            cdt.directed_edge(pair[0]).to().fix(),
+                            cdt.directed_edge(pair[1]).from().fix()
+                        );
+                    }
+                    for &edge in &edges {
+                        assert!(cdt.is_constraint_edge(edge.as_undirected()));
+                    }
+                }
+            }
+        }
+
+        let num_flagged = cdt
+            .undirected_edges()
+            .filter(|edge| edge.is_constraint_edge())
+            .count();
+        assert_eq!(num_flagged, cdt.num_constraints());
+
+        cdt.cdt_sanity_check();
+    }
 }
diff --git a/src/delaunay_core/math.rs b/src/delaunay_core/math.rs
index 0f94302..8b8c261 100644
--- a/src/delaunay_core/math.rs
+++ b/src/delaunay_core/math.rs
@@ -638,4 +638,105 @@ mod test {
             Point2::new(0f64, -0.5f64)
         ));
     }
+
+    // === Hegel property based tests ===
+
+    use super::{mitigate_underflow, validate_vertex, MAX_ALLOWED_VALUE, MIN_ALLOWED_VALUE};
+    use crate::{DelaunayTriangulation, Triangulation};
+    use alloc::vec;
+    use hegel::generators::{self as gens, Generator as _};
+    use std::{format, string::ToString};
+
+    /// Draws a completely arbitrary `f64` coordinate: any float (including NaN and the
+    /// infinities), boosted with the exact documented boundary values of the coordinate
+    /// contract and their closest neighbors just outside the allowed range.
+    fn draw_any_coordinate(tc: &hegel::TestCase) -> f64 {
+        tc.draw(hegel::one_of!(
+            gens::floats::<f64>().boxed(),
+            gens::sampled_from(vec![
+                0.0,
+                -0.0,
+                // The documented boundary values are valid ...
+                MIN_ALLOWED_VALUE,
+                -MIN_ALLOWED_VALUE,
+                MAX_ALLOWED_VALUE,
+                -MAX_ALLOWED_VALUE,
+                // ... their closest neighbors towards zero/infinity are not.
+                MIN_ALLOWED_VALUE.next_down(),
+                -MIN_ALLOWED_VALUE.next_up(),
+                MAX_ALLOWED_VALUE.next_up(),
+                -MAX_ALLOWED_VALUE.next_down(),
+                // Other classic extreme values.
+                f64::MIN_POSITIVE,
+                5e-324,
+                f64::MAX,
+                f64::MIN,
+                f64::INFINITY,
+                f64::NEG_INFINITY,
+                f64::NAN,
+            ])
+            .boxed(),
+        ))
+    }
+
+    /// `Triangulation::insert` accepts a vertex exactly if `validate_vertex` does (documented
+    /// on `bulk_load`/`insert`), returns the same error, never panics - and leaves the
+    /// triangulation unchanged when it fails (documented on `insert`).
+    #[hegel::test]
+    fn hegel_insert_agrees_with_validate_vertex(tc: hegel::TestCase) {
+        let mut triangulation = DelaunayTriangulation::<Point2<f64>>::new();
+        for point in [
+            Point2::new(0.0, 0.0),
+            Point2::new(1.0, 0.0),
+            Point2::new(0.0, 1.0),
+        ] {
+            triangulation.insert(point).unwrap();
+        }
+
+        let point = Point2::new(draw_any_coordinate(&tc), draw_any_coordinate(&tc));
+        let expected = validate_vertex(&point);
+
+        let vertices_before = triangulation.num_vertices();
+        let edges_before = triangulation.num_undirected_edges();
+        let result = triangulation.insert(point);
+
+        match expected {
+            Ok(()) => {
+                let handle = result.unwrap();
+                assert_eq!(triangulation.vertex(handle).position(), point);
+            }
+            Err(error) => {
+                assert_eq!(result, Err(error));
+                assert_eq!(triangulation.num_vertices(), vertices_before);
+                assert_eq!(triangulation.num_undirected_edges(), edges_before);
+            }
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// A position returned by `mitigate_underflow` never causes `InsertionError::TooSmall`
+    /// (documented on `mitigate_underflow`), and the function only ever flushes
+    /// too-small coordinates to zero - all other coordinates pass through unchanged.
+    #[hegel::test]
+    fn hegel_mitigate_underflow_never_too_small(tc: hegel::TestCase) {
+        let point = Point2::new(draw_any_coordinate(&tc), draw_any_coordinate(&tc));
+        let mitigated = mitigate_underflow(point);
+
+        for (original, result) in [(point.x, mitigated.x), (point.y, mitigated.y)] {
+            if original.is_nan() {
+                assert!(result.is_nan());
+                continue;
+            }
+            assert_ne!(
+                validate_coordinate(result),
+                Err(InsertionError::TooSmall),
+                "mitigate_underflow left a too-small coordinate behind: \
+                 {original} became {result}"
+            );
+            if result != original {
+                // Rounds towards zero, and only for coordinates in the underflow band.
+                assert_eq!(result, 0.0);
+                assert!(original != 0.0 && original.abs() < MIN_ALLOWED_VALUE);
+            }
+        }
+    }
 }
